Task 1: Configure the OADP Operator for Backup Tasks
Subtask 1.1: Install the OADP Operator
First, we'll install the OpenShift APIs for Data Protection operator which provides backup capabilities through Velero.

Access your OpenShift cluster:
# Verify cluster access
oc whoami
oc get nodes
Create the OADP operator namespace:
# Create namespace for OADP
oc create namespace openshift-adp
Install the OADP operator using the CLI:
# Create operator subscription
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: redhat-oadp-operator
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  channel: stable-1.3
  name: redhat-oadp-operator
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
EOF
Verify the operator installation:
# Check operator status
oc get csv -n openshift-adp
oc get pods -n openshift-adp
Subtask 1.2: Configure Storage for Backups
We'll set up local storage for backup destinations. In production environments, you would typically use cloud storage like AWS S3 or Azure Blob Storage.

Create a storage location for backups:
# Create persistent volume for backup storage
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: backup-storage-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 50Gi
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain
  storageClassName: local-storage
  hostPath:
    path: /opt/backup-storage
    type: DirectoryOrCreate
EOF
Create a persistent volume claim:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: backup-storage-pvc
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 50Gi
  storageClassName: local-storage
EOF
Subtask 1.3: Configure the DataProtectionApplication
Create the DataProtectionApplication resource:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1
kind: DataProtectionApplication
metadata:
  name: velero-dpa
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  configuration:
    velero:
      defaultPlugins:
        - openshift
        - aws
        - csi
      resourceTimeout: 10m
    restic:
      enable: true
      podVolumeOperationTimeout: 60m
  backupLocations:
    - velero:
        config:
          region: us-east-1
          s3ForcePathStyle: "true"
          s3Url: http://minio.openshift-adp.svc:9000
        credential:
          key: cloud
          name: cloud-credentials
        default: true
        objectStorage:
          bucket: velero-backups
          prefix: backups
        provider: aws
EOF
Create credentials for backup storage (using MinIO for local testing):
# Create MinIO deployment for local object storage
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: minio
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: minio
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: minio
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: minio
        image: quay.io/minio/minio:latest
        command:
        - /bin/bash
        - -c
        args:
        - minio server /data --console-address :9001
        env:
        - name: MINIO_ACCESS_KEY
          value: "minio"
        - name: MINIO_SECRET_KEY
          value: "minio123"
        ports:
        - containerPort: 9000
        - containerPort: 9001
        volumeMounts:
        - name: storage
          mountPath: /data
      volumes:
      - name: storage
        emptyDir: {}
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: minio
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  selector:
    app: minio
  ports:
  - name: api
    port: 9000
    targetPort: 9000
  - name: console
    port: 9001
    targetPort: 9001
EOF
Create cloud credentials secret:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: cloud-credentials
  namespace: openshift-adp
type: Opaque
stringData:
  cloud: |
    [default]
    aws_access_key_id=minio
    aws_secret_access_key=minio123
EOF
Verify OADP installation:
# Check if Velero pods are running
oc get pods -n openshift-adp
oc get dpa -n openshift-adp
Task 2: Perform a Backup of a VM Using Velero
Subtask 2.1: Create a Sample Virtual Machine
First, let's create a virtual machine that we can backup.

Create a test virtual machine:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: test-vm-backup
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: test-vm
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: test-vm-backup
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - masquerade: {}
            name: default
        machine:
          type: pc-q35-rhel8.4.0
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
            bootcmd:
              - echo "Test data for backup" > /home/cirros/test-file.txt
        name: cloudinitdisk
EOF
Verify the VM is running:
# Check VM status
oc get vm test-vm-backup
oc get vmi test-vm-backup
Create some test data in the VM:
# Wait for VM to be ready
oc wait --for=condition=Ready vmi/test-vm-backup --timeout=300s

# Connect to the VM and create test data
virtctl console test-vm-backup
# Inside the VM, run:
# echo "Important backup test data" > /tmp/backup-test.txt
# echo "Configuration data" > /etc/test-config.conf
# exit (to disconnect from console)
Subtask 2.2: Create a Backup Policy
Create a backup configuration:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Backup
metadata:
  name: vm-backup-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  includedNamespaces:
  - default
  labelSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: test-vm
  storageLocation: velero-dpa-1
  ttl: 720h0m0s
  includedResources:
  - virtualmachines
  - virtualmachineinstances
  - datavolumes
  - persistentvolumeclaims
  - persistentvolumes
EOF
Create a scheduled backup for regular operations:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Schedule
metadata:
  name: vm-daily-backup
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"  # Daily at 2 AM
  template:
    includedNamespaces:
    - default
    labelSelector:
      matchLabels:
        app: test-vm
    storageLocation: velero-dpa-1
    ttl: 168h0m0s  # 7 days retention
    includedResources:
    - virtualmachines
    - virtualmachineinstances
    - datavolumes
    - persistentvolumeclaims
EOF
Subtask 2.3: Execute the Backup
Trigger an immediate backup:
# Create backup using Velero CLI
velero backup create vm-manual-backup \
  --include-namespaces default \
  --selector app=test-vm \
  --wait
Monitor backup progress:
# Check backup status
oc get backup -n openshift-adp
velero backup describe vm-manual-backup
velero backup logs vm-manual-backup
Verify backup completion:
# List all backups
velero backup get
oc get backup -n openshift-adp -o wide
Task 3: Restore the VM from Backup and Verify Data Integrity
Subtask 3.1: Simulate Data Loss
To demonstrate the restore process, we'll simulate a disaster scenario.

Delete the original virtual machine:
# Stop the VM first
oc patch vm test-vm-backup --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":false}}'

# Wait for VM to stop
oc wait --for=delete vmi/test-vm-backup --timeout=120s

# Delete the VM completely
oc delete vm test-vm-backup
oc delete pvc -l app=test-vm  # Delete associated storage
Verify the VM is completely removed:
# Confirm deletion
oc get vm test-vm-backup
oc get vmi test-vm-backup
oc get pvc -l app=test-vm
Subtask 3.2: Perform the Restore Operation
Create a restore from the backup:
# Restore from the most recent backup
velero restore create vm-restore-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S) \
  --from-backup vm-manual-backup \
  --wait
Monitor the restore process:
# Check restore status
velero restore get
velero restore describe vm-restore-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
Alternative restore using YAML:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Restore
metadata:
  name: vm-restore-yaml
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  backupName: vm-manual-backup
  includedNamespaces:
  - default
  restorePVs: true
EOF
Subtask 3.3: Verify Data Integrity
Check if the VM was restored successfully:
# Verify VM restoration
oc get vm test-vm-backup
oc get vmi test-vm-backup
oc get pvc -l app=test-vm
Start the restored VM:
# Start the VM if it's not running
oc patch vm test-vm-backup --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'

# Wait for VM to be ready
oc wait --for=condition=Ready vmi/test-vm-backup --timeout=300s
Verify data integrity inside the VM:
# Connect to the restored VM
virtctl console test-vm-backup

# Inside the VM, verify the test data:
# cat /tmp/backup-test.txt
# cat /etc/test-config.conf
# ls -la /home/cirros/test-file.txt
Compare VM configuration:
# Export current VM configuration
oc get vm test-vm-backup -o yaml > restored-vm-config.yaml

# Check resource allocation and settings
oc describe vm test-vm-backup
Subtask 3.4: Advanced Restore Scenarios
Restore to a different namespace:
# Create target namespace
oc create namespace vm-restore-target

# Restore with namespace mapping
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Restore
metadata:
  name: vm-restore-different-ns
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  backupName: vm-manual-backup
  namespaceMapping:
    default: vm-restore-target
  includedNamespaces:
  - default
EOF
Selective resource restore:
# Restore only specific resources
velero restore create vm-selective-restore \
  --from-backup vm-manual-backup \
  --include-resources virtualmachines,persistentvolumeclaims \
  --wait
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Backup Fails with Storage Errors
Symptoms: Backup status shows "Failed" with storage-related errors.

Solution:

# Check storage configuration
oc get backupstoragelocation -n openshift-adp
oc describe backupstoragelocation -n openshift-adp

# Verify MinIO connectivity
oc port-forward svc/minio 9000:9000 -n openshift-adp
# Test connection to localhost:9000
Issue 2: VM Fails to Start After Restore
Symptoms: Restored VM shows "Failed" or "Pending" status.

Solution:

# Check VM events
oc describe vm test-vm-backup
oc get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp

# Verify storage classes
oc get storageclass
oc get pvc -o wide
Issue 3: Incomplete Data Restoration
Symptoms: VM starts but some data is missing.

Solution:

# Check if all PVCs were restored
oc get pvc -l app=test-vm
oc describe pvc <pvc-name>

# Verify backup included all necessary resources
velero backup describe vm-manual-backup --details
Best Practices for VM Backup and Restore
Backup Strategy Recommendations
Regular Scheduled Backups:
# Create weekly full backups
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Schedule
metadata:
  name: vm-weekly-full-backup
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  schedule: "0 1 * * 0"  # Weekly on Sunday at 1 AM
  template:
    includedNamespaces:
    - default
    - production-vms
    storageLocation: velero-dpa-1
    ttl: 2160h0m0s  # 90 days retention
EOF
Application-Consistent Backups:
# Use pre and post hooks for application consistency
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Backup
metadata:
  name: vm-app-consistent-backup
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  includedNamespaces:
  - default
  hooks:
    resources:
    - name: vm-pre-backup-hook
      includedNamespaces:
      - default
      labelSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: test-vm
      pre:
      - exec:
          command:
          - /bin/bash
          - -c
          - "sync && echo 'Pre-backup sync completed'"
          container: compute
EOF
Monitoring and Alerting
Create backup monitoring script:
#!/bin/bash
# backup-monitor.sh
BACKUP_NAME=$1
STATUS=$(velero backup get $BACKUP_NAME -o jsonpath='{.status.phase}')

if [ "$STATUS" = "Completed" ]; then
    echo "Backup $BACKUP_NAME completed successfully"
    exit 0
elif [ "$STATUS" = "Failed" ]; then
    echo "Backup $BACKUP_NAME failed"
    velero backup logs $BACKUP_NAME
    exit 1
else
    echo "Backup $BACKUP_NAME is in progress: $STATUS"
fi
